==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|ulage}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|ulliage}}, from {{m|fro|*ullier||to fill a partially empty cask}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|oel||bunghole", literally, "eye}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|oculus||eye}}. See French {{m|fr|ouillage}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# In a wine bottle, the empty space between the cork and the top of the wine.
# In a cask or barrel, the empty space, occupied by air, that is created by not completely filling the cask or barrel.
# The topping-up of such a barrel with fresh wine.
# In an industrial setting, the empty space in a tank, such as for fuel.
#Spillage from barrels (this was how I learnt the word whilst working on Shoreham docks in the 1970s).
#What has been "liberated" by the dockers.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|ullag}}

# To [[gauge]] the amount of empty space between the top of a cask and the level of liquid inside it.